# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.venv/
venv/

# Tooling
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Local secrets / session (never commit) — belt-and-braces; the real store
# lives in ~/.super-agent, not in-repo, but guard against accidents.
.super-agent/
*.session.json
session.json

# Logs (the bridge log lives in ~/.super-agent, outside the repo; guard anyway)
*.log

# Generated per-chat streaming-watchdog shims + their de-dup state. The runtime
# writes sr_poll_<platform>_<hash>.py (baked chat_id) next to the real scripts,
# and the watchdog writes .sr_*state.json — never commit these (the published
# wheel ships only sr.py + sr_attention_poll.py; see pyproject package-data).
facade/skill/scripts/sr_poll_*.py
facade/skill/scripts/.sr_*.json

# ⛔⛔ RUNTIME STATE THIS PACKAGE WRITES INTO ITS OWN TREE WHEN RUN FROM A
# CHECKOUT. All four were sitting here untracked and unignored, so a single
# `git add -A` would have committed them — and two of them carry real content:
# `prefs.json` holds the signed-in ACCOUNT's label, install id and runtime, and
# `bridge_launcher.py` is generated with an absolute pipx path from whoever ran
# it (a `/home/<user>/...` string, useless and identifying on any other machine).
# `podcasts/` is downloaded media and `.version_check.json` is a PyPI-poll cache.
#
# ⛔ NONE OF THESE BELONG TO THE PACKAGE. The published wheel ships from
# pyproject's explicit file list, so ignoring them cannot affect what is built —
# it only stops the repo swallowing a developer's own account state.
prefs.json
bridge_launcher.py
.version_check.json
podcasts/
